  "nameStory": "The ground cherry sweat bee gets its name from its close association with ground cherry plants, where it's commonly found foraging for nectar and pollen. Like all sweat bees, it earned the 'sweat' part of its name from its habit of landing on people to sip the salt from their perspiration on hot summer days.",
  "description": "The ground cherry sweat bee is a small, dark-colored native bee that belongs to the diverse sweat bee family. These compact bees typically measure about a quarter to half an inch long and have a somewhat metallic sheen to their dark bodies. Unlike honeybees, they're solitary creatures that nest in the ground, with females doing all the work of digging burrows and provisioning their young. They're important pollinators of wildflowers and garden plants throughout New England.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Size and build",
      "description": "Small and compact, roughly the size of your pinky nail, with a sturdy, somewhat stocky appearance compared to other small bees",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Body color",
      "description": "Dark brownish-black body with a subtle metallic gleam that catches the light, especially visible when the bee moves in bright sunlight",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Behavior",
      "description": "Often seen hovering low over bare soil or visiting ground cherry flowers; may land on your arm or hand to sample sweat on warm days",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Flight pattern",
      "description": "Quick, darting flight with frequent stops at flowers; less deliberate than honeybee flight but more purposeful than hover flies",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Nesting signs",
      "description": "Small, pencil-eraser-sized holes in bare or sparsely vegetated soil, often with tiny piles of excavated dirt nearby",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Look for ground cherry sweat bees in gardens, old fields, and forest edges where ground cherry plants grow, particularly in areas with well-drained, sandy or loamy soil suitable for nesting. They prefer sunny to partially shaded locations with a mix of bare ground for nesting and flowering plants for foraging. You'll often find them in disturbed areas like trail edges, vacant lots, and the margins of cultivated areas.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Adults emerge from underground nests in late spring, with females immediately beginning to excavate new burrows in suitable soil.",
    "summer": "Peak activity period when you'll see them most frequently visiting flowers and males patrolling for mates. Fresh nest holes appear in bare soil.",
    "fall": "Activity gradually decreases as new generation prepares for winter dormancy. Late-season flowers may still attract foraging individuals.",
    "winter": "Adults overwinter in underground burrows, completely inactive and hidden from view until spring warmth triggers emergence."
  },
  "funFact": "Ground cherry sweat bees are such efficient diggers that a single female can excavate a burrow up to 8 inches deep in just one day, using her powerful mandibles to cut through soil and her legs to kick out the debris like a tiny construction crew of one!",
  "confusionNotes": "Most easily confused with other dark sweat bees in the same genus, but the ground cherry sweat bee's preference for ground cherry flowers and its specific metallic sheen help distinguish it. Remember: if it's sipping from ground cherries and has that subtle metallic gleam, you've likely found your species.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "The genus name Lasioglossum means 'hairy tongue' in Greek, referring to the fuzzy structure these bees use to lap up nectar. Despite being called 'sweat bees,' they're actually beneficial pollinators that rarely sting and only land on people for the minerals in perspiration.",
  "compareHints": "Among its many Lasioglossum relatives in New England, the ground cherry sweat bee is distinguished by its strong association with ground cherry plants and its particular shade of dark metallic coloring. While other species like the deep-blue sweat bee have more obvious metallic blue-green hues, the ground cherry sweat bee shows a more subdued brownish-metallic sheen. Its habitat preferences also differ from coastal species like the marine-associated Lasioglossum oceanicum.",
